---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ConversionActions from '../components/ConversionActions.astro';
import InvestigationDeliverables from '../components/InvestigationDeliverables.astro';
import InvestigationFAQ from '../components/InvestigationFAQ.astro';
import { site } from '../config/site';
---
<BaseLayout title="PostgreSQL Performance Investigation for RDS & Aurora | WalStack" description="Find the cause of a slow or expensive PostgreSQL database. Get a fix, rollback steps and a 14-day check. $450 for my first 3 clients, then $750."
  schema={{ '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Service', name: 'Postgres Slowdown Investigation', url: site.url + '/postgresql-performance-investigation', provider: { '@id': site.url + '/#practice' } }}>
  <header class="container hero"><p class="eyebrow">PostgreSQL performance</p><h1>Postgres Slowdown Investigation</h1>
    <p class="lede">Find out why pages slowed down or the database bill went up. Get a written fix your engineers can test and undo.</p>
    <p>{site.investigationOffer.display}. Paid upfront.</p><ConversionActions offer="investigation" /></header>
  <section><div class="container prose"><h2>Who it's for</h2>
    <p>You run one production database on RDS, Aurora or self-managed PostgreSQL. Something changed, and the engineer who knows the database needs a second pair of eyes.</p>
    <p>If it's just one slow query or endpoint, say so in the <a href="/snapshot#send">snapshot form</a> because the investigation covers it. If you can already name the statement and only want that query reviewed, <a href="/query-rescue">Query Rescue</a> is narrower. For a change that hasn't shipped yet, see <a href="/database-code-review">Database Code Review</a>.</p>
    <h2>What you get</h2><InvestigationDeliverables />
    <p><a href="/sample-report" data-track="sample_report_click">Read the sample report</a>. I built it on a demo database.</p>
    <h2>How the three business days work</h2>
    <p>The clock starts when I have read-only access or the output of scripts you run. You keep control of production changes.</p>
    <ol><li><strong>Day 1.</strong> I match the symptom to a time window. I compare query timings with traffic, database activity and recent releases.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 2.</strong> I check which explanations fit. If two causes look alike, I ask for the smallest extra check that could distinguish them.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 3.</strong> I send the report with the proposed SQL, risks, rollback steps and before-and-after checks. You get a 45-minute review call.</li></ol>
    <p>At the 14-day follow-up, I compare results with the original symptom and explain what changed.</p>
    <h2>What can the data prove?</h2>
    <p>Two captures can show that one query accounted for most of the extra database time. A single total can't tell me when the increase began.</p>
    <p>If history is missing or the problem didn't recur, I say so. I explain which next measurement would settle the question.</p>
    <h2>Price and refund</h2><p><strong>{site.investigationOffer.display}.</strong> Paid upfront in USD. If I can't find at least one evidence-backed improvement, you get a full refund.</p>
    <p>WalStack is <a href={site.directoryUrl}>listed in PostgreSQL.org's Professional Services directory</a>.</p>
    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><InvestigationFAQ /><ConversionActions offer="investigation" />
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
